# sdkconfig overlay applied ONLY to -DTR_BOARD_V9=1 builds, on top of
# sdkconfig.defaults. Selected by the project CMakeLists, not by the build-dir
# name — see there.
#
# This file sets NOTHING. It is the V9 per-board hook — the same shape as
# out_computer/sdkconfig.defaults.v9 and as the M1 overlay next to it, which
# exist because sdkconfig is per-project while some settings are per-BOARD —
# and it currently has no such setting to carry. It stays so a V9-only
# setting has a home when one is needed, and so the CMake status line can say
# in one place what the overlay does and does not do.

# === Boot NOR: no longer declared here (#916, owner decision 2026-09-09) ===
# Until #1314 this file carried CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_16MB=y, because U16
# (the P4's boot flash) is a 16 MB GD25Q128ESIG on V9/V10 where everything
# fabbed before the 2026-08-09 close-out carries an 8 MB W25Q64JVXGIQ:
# hardware/rocket-computer/bom.csv has the swap at 8f87aa0, and the V7 board
# in hand measures ef/7017 = 8 MB on both MCUs (2026-08-24).
#
# That per-board declaration is gone. Every project now declares the SMALLEST
# standard size that fits its own partition table, not the part fitted, and
# tinkerrocket-idf/tools/check_flash_declarations.py fails the Docs job on any
# headroom above the table — including a 16 MB line put back here.
# partitions.csv ends at 0x610000 (6.06 MB), so the 8 MB in sdkconfig.defaults
# covers every board this project builds for (V7, V8, V9, M1), and a V9 build
# generates CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE="8MB".
#
# What that costs a V9 is deliberate: it logs "Detected size(16384k) larger
# than the size in the binary image header(8192k)" on every boot and the top
# half of the part is unaddressable. Under-declaring costs only that;
# over-declaring is the direction that boot-loops an 8 MB V7/V8 inside the
# bootloader, with no firmware log at all — the V7 boot loop of 2026-08-24 is
# how #916 started. If a partition ever crosses 8 MB, grow the table first and
# then raise the base declaration to the next standard size — that is the
# workflow the guard enforces, not a bypass.
